  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 150 PM CDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\nCurrently northwest flow over the Northern Plains is present as\napart of a large cyclonic system in south central Canada. This\ncyclonic flow has brought much cooler temperatures to the \nregion and has allowed diurnal cumulus to form. This has also \nkept the wildfire smoke from the Pacific Northwest to stay out \nof North Dakota, and instead flow to the south. For tonight we\nbump down the NBM lows about 4 degrees as last night we dropped\nwell below the NBM forecast.\n\nThrough the rest of this week, a ridge that is currently over\nthe Pacific Northwest will slowly move east. A thermal ridge\nbuilt into the ridge will aid in warming temperatures back into\nthe low 90s, mainly southwest and south central, by Thursday.\nHeading into the weekend that ridge will flatten and push the\nmuch the warmer temperatures in the thermal ridge south of the\nstate. Highs over the weekend will then be steady in the 70s to\n80s. Fire weather concerns continue to increase, mainly in the\nsouthwest half of the state, with warm temperatures and relative\nhumidities 15 to 20 percent, along with drying grasses. \n\nEarly next week there will be a low to medium chance of rain\nstarting Sunday night with a small wave in the cyclonic flow in\nsouthern Canada. That cyclonic flow looks to stick around next \nweek and regulate our temperatures around average or slightly \nbelow (low 80s).",
